# Add a repo to {{.Name}}

1. Pick a name: lowercase letters, digits, `-` and `_`. It becomes the
   compose service, the `repos/<name>` folder and `http://<name>.localhost`.
2. See the stacks and their options:

   ```sh
   neptuno stacks
   ```

3. Add it. Pass every choice as a flag, since without a terminal Neptuno
   can't show its option checklist:

   ```sh
   neptuno add <name> <git-url> --stack <stack>                      # an existing repo, as a submodule
   neptuno add <name> --stack <stack> --with <opt> --without <opt>   # a new app from the stack
   neptuno add <name>                                                # a blank repo
   ```

   Add `--wildcard` when the app serves one subdomain per tenant, and
   `--port <port>` when its dev server doesn't listen on the stack's port.
   `add` never overwrites files, so it is safe to run again after a
   failure.
4. Start it and check it:

   ```sh
   neptuno up -d <name>
   neptuno status
   docker compose logs --tail 100 <name>
   ```

   The first start of a Rails app runs `bundle install` and
   `db:prepare` in `entrypoint.sh`, which takes a while; follow the logs
   until its processes run.
5. Open `http://<name>.localhost` to check it answers. If it doesn't, use
   the `neptuno-debug-service` skill.
6. If the app serves an MCP server, declare it in `neptuno.yml`:

   ```yaml
   repos:
     <name>:
       mcp:
         <server>: /path/to/mcp
   ```

   then run `neptuno mcp sync` and check it with `neptuno mcp`.
7. Commit the new files in this project (`compose.yml`, `Caddyfile`,
   `dockerfiles/<name>`, `procfiles/<name>`, `environments/<name>`,
   `.gitmodules` and the `repos/<name>` submodule). The app's own code is
   committed inside `repos/<name>`.
